SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Completes the Combined Seventh, and Eighth Reviews of the Extended Fund Facility for Pakistan","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2022-08-29","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/08/29/pr22293-imf-executive-board-completes-reviews-of-extended-fund-facility-pakistan","claim":"The IMF said the authorities had taken important measures and the immediate priorities were FY23 budget implementation, a market-determined exchange rate, prudent monetary policy, expanded social safety, and SOE governance reform.","locator":"lines 240-247 and 251-256","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official board decision and staff appraisal with specific policy priorities and quantified program context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late August 2022, directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to Sharif's government, though much was driven by inherited imbalances and external financing stress.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also said FY22 policy was procyclical and unsustainable.","Corrective action indicates prior mismanagement rather than clean stewardship."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.6","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- The World Bank’s April baseline frames what Sharif inherited rather than what he created.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Long-standing Structural Challenges Pose Risks to Pakistan’s Sustained Growth: World Bank Bi-Annual Pakistan Development Update Report","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-04-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/04/19/long-standing-structural-challenges-pose-risks-to-pakistan-s-sustained-growth-world-bank-bi-annual-pakistan-development-","claim":"The World Bank said Pakistan already faced double-digit inflation, a sharp rise in imports, rupee pressure, and low investment, exports, and productivity before Sharif’s economic team settled in.","locator":"lines 136-143","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary multilateral assessment at the start of the period.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"April 2022 baseline immediately before and after Sharif took office.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly inherited conditions rather than Sharif's own policy record.","contrary_evidence":["This is baseline context, not direct evidence of Sharif's choices.","Some pressures predate his government."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.10"]}

- By October, the World Bank was projecting weak growth, high inflation, and rising poverty because of floods and macro stress.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank: Pakistan’s Economy Slows Down While Inflation Rises Amid Catastrophic Floods","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/06/world-bank-pakistan-s-economy-slows-down-while-inflation-rises-amid-catastrophic-floods","claim":"The World Bank projected only 2 percent growth for FY23, around 23 percent inflation, and a worsening poverty rate because of floods, tight money, high debt, and weak external conditions.","locator":"lines 135-140","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank update with quantified macro and welfare projections.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"October 2022 within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Partly inherited and shock-driven; Sharif’s response mattered in cushioning impact and adjusting policy.","contrary_evidence":["The deterioration was heavily driven by floods and global conditions.","This is more an outcome/constraint source than a clean ruler-performance source."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- The flood assessment quantified the scale of the disaster and the poverty shock.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan: Flood Damages and Economic Losses Over USD 30 billion and Reconstruction Needs Over USD 16 billion - New Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/28/pakistan-flood-damages-and-economic-losses-over-usd-30-billion-and-reconstruction-needs-over-usd-16-billion-new-assessme","claim":"The PDNA estimated damages above USD 14.9 billion, losses about USD 15.2 billion, reconstruction needs of at least USD 16.3 billion, and a poverty increase of 3.7 to 4.0 percentage points affecting 8.4 to 9.1 million more people.","locator":"lines 140-147","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint World Bank/Pakistan assessment with quantified losses and poverty impacts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Largely shock-driven and inherited-exposure context rather than pure policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["The report is about disaster damage, not direct economic-policy competence.","It limits what can fairly be credited to Sharif."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Sharif personally expanded flood relief through BISP.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM announces to increase BISP flood relief aid to Rs70b","publisher":"Radio Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-09-05","url":"https://www.radio.gov.pk/05-09-2022/pm-announces-to-increase-bisp-flood-relief-aid-to-rs70b","claim":"Sharif said flood relief assistance under BISP would rise from Rs28 billion to Rs70 billion and each affected family would receive Rs25,000.","locator":"lines 34-39","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PM announcement carried by the state broadcaster.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 flood-response period.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to Sharif personally.","contrary_evidence":["Emergency cash relief is necessary but temporary.","It does not prove durable income recovery or structural reform."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}

- The government and UN jointly launched the flood response plan and described immediate cash relief.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Media Update: United Nations Pakistan, 30 August 2022","publisher":"United Nations in Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-08-30","url":"https://pakistan.un.org/en/197087-media-update-united-nations-pakistan-30-august-2022","claim":"The Government of Pakistan and the UN jointly launched the 2022 Floods Response Plan, and the UN said the government had released national funds including immediate cash relief.","locator":"lines 64-72 and 76-80","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN country-office press release summarizing the joint response and cash relief.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"August 2022 flood-response period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Sharif's government but mediated through disaster-response machinery and international partners.","contrary_evidence":["This is humanitarian response rather than long-run prosperity.","Much of the spending was forced by disaster rather than discretionary reform."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}

- UN Women’s flood response adds a distributional and gender-specific dimension.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN Women puts women and girls at the heart of flood response in Pakistan","publisher":"United Nations in Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-09-07","url":"https://pakistan.un.org/en/198187-un-women-puts-women-and-girls-heart-flood-response-pakistan","claim":"UN Women distributed relief items and cash grants to vulnerable women and planned women’s centres and GBV-response services for flood-affected areas.","locator":"lines 62-66","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN reporting with specific interventions for vulnerable groups and women.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"September 2022, directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to the government’s disaster-response environment rather than uniquely to Sharif.","contrary_evidence":["This is relief rather than long-run prosperity.","It shows crisis mitigation more than structural reform."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I screened roughly 25-40 documents across Reuters, IMF, World Bank, Dawn, Radio Pakistan, UN country pages, APP, Euronews, and a few later retrospectives. I kept the set above because it best separated inherited crisis from Sharif-era choices and added both favorable and contrary evidence.

I rejected or did not use:
- Duplicate Reuters/Investing/Euronews copies of the same IMF or budget stories when they added no new facts.
- Later 2023-2026 summaries that were out of period for the 2022 chapter.
- Broad macro pages that only repeated baseline indicators already covered by the local prior.
- Paywall/cache-missed or low-yield items that could not be extracted reliably.
- Source pages on unrelated foreign-policy or security topics from other chapters.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct evidence on how much Sharif personally drove economic appointments versus party-family preferences remains only partial.
- Implementation evidence for anti-corruption, SOE, and governance reforms is still stronger on commitments than on completed outcomes.
- Household-level distribution data for late 2022, especially by region and class, would improve the lens on fairness and trajectory.
- More search would help mainly on appointment/implementation detail; it is unlikely to change the macro-shock picture materially.

